**Proof.**

The answer is **no**. We construct a $T_0$ (indeed $T_1$) totally disconnected space that admits no base of closed (i.e. clopen) sets.

**The space.** Let
$$X = \{p, q\} \cup \{x_n : n \in \mathbb{N}\},$$
where $p,q$ and the $x_n$ are pairwise distinct. Topologize $X$ by declaring every $x_n$ isolated, and declaring the neighborhoods of $p$ to be all sets containing $\{p\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge N\}$ for some $N\in\mathbb{N}$, and the neighborhoods of $q$ to be all sets containing $\{q\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge N\}$ for some $N\in\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently, $U\subseteq X$ is open iff

- whenever $p\in U$, there is $N$ with $\{x_n:n\ge N\}\subseteq U$;
- whenever $q\in U$, there is $N$ with $\{x_n:n\ge N\}\subseteq U$.

(No condition is imposed at the isolated points $x_n$.) This is a topology: arbitrary unions and finite intersections preserve the "tail" condition at $p$ and $q$.

**$X$ is $T_1$ (hence $T_0$).** Each singleton is closed. Indeed $\{x_n\}$ is open (isolated), and its complement $X\setminus\{x_n\}$ is open since it contains $p$ (resp. $q$) together with a tail $\{x_m:m\ge n+1\}$, and contains every other $x_m$ as an isolated point. Also $\{p\}$ is closed because $X\setminus\{p\}=\{q\}\cup\{x_n:n\in\mathbb{N}\}$ is open (it contains $q$ and the whole tail $\{x_n:n\ge 1\}$), and symmetrically $\{q\}$ is closed. Thus $X$ is $T_1$.

**$X$ is totally disconnected.** Let $A\subseteq X$ be connected. If $A$ contains some $x_n$ together with any other point, then since $\{x_n\}$ is clopen in $X$, the set $A\cap\{x_n\}=\{x_n\}$ is a nonempty proper clopen subset of $A$, contradicting the connectedness of $A$. Hence any connected $A$ with at least two points must lie inside $\{p,q\}$. But $\{p,q\}$ is discrete in its subspace topology:
$$\{p\}=\bigl(\{p\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge 1\}\bigr)\cap\{p,q\},\qquad \{q\}=\bigl(\{q\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge 1\}\bigr)\cap\{p,q\},$$
both open in the subspace. So $\{p,q\}$ is disconnected. Therefore every connected subset of $X$ is a singleton: $X$ is totally disconnected.

**$X$ has no base of closed sets.** Suppose $\mathcal B$ were a base of $X$ consisting of closed sets; since every base element is open, each $B\in\mathcal B$ would be clopen. Consider the open neighborhood $U=\{p\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge 1\}=X\setminus\{q\}$ of $p$. There must exist $B\in\mathcal B$ with $p\in B\subseteq U$. Since $B$ is open and $p\in B$, we have $\{p\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge N\}\subseteq B$ for some $N$. Since $B\subseteq U$, we have $q\notin B$. Since $B$ is closed, $X\setminus B$ is open; and $q\in X\setminus B$ forces $\{q\}\cup\{x_n:n\ge M\}\subseteq X\setminus B$ for some $M$. But then
$$\{x_n:n\ge \max(N,M)\}\subseteq B\cap(X\setminus B)=\varnothing,$$
a contradiction. So no clopen base — and hence no base of closed sets — exists.

We have exhibited a $T_0$ totally disconnected space with no base consisting of closed sets. Therefore the implication fails in general. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
